 The LwM2M Firmware Update object (Object 5), wired to the metal.

 OtaUpdate has had the state machine for a while and it is pure by
 design: ota-step signals ActionWriteBlock and performs nothing. Board
 grew a linear FlashBank on 2026-07-16. Coap grew a parser beside this
 change. This chapter is the binding layer between the three -- the part
 that was missing, and the reason the firmware-update story was a
 diagram. A server writes the Package URI, blocks arrive as CoAP
 responses, and each one lands in the staging bank through the Flash
 effect. Nothing here invents a byte.

 What this chapter is NOT: a socket. CoAP runs over UDP and codex-vm
 serves exactly one UDP port (53, DNS) and drops the rest, so no guest
 can complete a live CoAP exchange today. That is BACKLOG 5.4 and a
 missing VM capability, not a gap in this flow. So the transport is
 injected: fw-feed-response takes the bytes of a CoAP response from
 wherever the caller got them. Every byte of protocol handling above the
 socket is real and is exercised; the socket is the one unproven inch,
 exactly as it is for cleartext fetch (1.7) and TLS (1.9).

 We say:

Section: Update Result Codes

 The standard Object 5 Update Result values, mapped as
 docs/Designs/Active/IoT/OTAFirmwareUpdate.md maps them. Result is the
 only place a failure survives: OMA says the device returns to state Idle
 after a failed update, so state alone cannot tell success from disaster
 and a reader that ignores Result learns nothing.

  fw-result-initial : Integer between 0 and 9 = 0
  fw-result-success : Integer between 0 and 9 = 1
  fw-result-no-flash : Integer between 0 and 9 = 2
  fw-result-out-of-ram : Integer between 0 and 9 = 3
  fw-result-conn-lost : Integer between 0 and 9 = 4
  fw-result-integrity : Integer between 0 and 9 = 5
  fw-result-bad-package : Integer between 0 and 9 = 6
  fw-result-invalid-uri : Integer between 0 and 9 = 7
  fw-result-update-failed : Integer between 0 and 9 = 8

Section: The Client

 fw-bank is the flash controller's register base; fw-stage is where the
 image lands. They are different addresses and conflating them is how a
 driver writes an image over its own controller.

 The staged image is never held in memory. Blocks go from the parsed
 response straight into the bank, and Gate A hashes the bank -- so what
 is verified is what actually landed, not what the client believes it
 sent. A write that never reached the silicon cannot pass this gate.

  Lwm2mFw = record {
   fw-ctx : OtaContext,
   fw-bank : Integer,
   fw-stage : Integer,
   fw-written : Integer,
   fw-next-block : Integer,
   fw-faults : Integer,
   fw-result : Integer between 0 and 9
  }

  fw-new : BootConfig, OtaVersion, Integer, Integer -> Lwm2mFw
  fw-new (boot) (version) (bank) (stage) = Lwm2mFw {
   fw-ctx = ota-context-new boot version,
   fw-bank = bank,
   fw-stage = stage,
   fw-written = 0,
   fw-next-block = 0,
   fw-faults = 0,
   fw-result = 0
  }

 Every result code enters the record through here. The bounds prover
 reads literals, field accesses and __narrow of those -- it does not read
 a named constant's declared range -- so a bare fw-result-integrity would
 be CDX2051 even though the constant is declared 0..9. Narrowing a
 parameter is the idiom that carries the assertion (ota-version-new does
 the same with a sequence number), and it costs one runtime check on a
 path that runs once per update.

 This was seven lines of hand-expanded record until 2026-07-16, because
 __narrow did not survive revised's hoist and the checker rejected the
 assertion the diagnostic recommended. That is fixed in the desugarer
 (codex/test/revised-narrow), so the update sugar reads as it should.

  fw-set-result : Lwm2mFw, Integer -> Lwm2mFw
  fw-set-result (c) (r) = c revised { fw-result = __narrow r }

  fw-fail : Lwm2mFw, Integer, Text -> Lwm2mFw
  fw-fail (c) (result) (why) =
   let ctx2 = ota-step (c.fw-ctx) (EvDownloadFailed why)
   in fw-set-result (c revised { fw-ctx = ctx2 }) result

Section: Object 5 Resources

 State is /5/0/3 and Result is /5/0/5. Both are reads a server performs
 to learn where the device is, and both are derived rather than stored --
 a second copy of the state is a second thing to get wrong.

  fw-state-resource : Lwm2mFw -> Integer
  fw-state-resource (c) =
   when c.fw-ctx.state
    is OtaIdle -> lwm2m-fw-state-value FwIdle
    is OtaDownloading (received) (total) -> lwm2m-fw-state-value FwDownloading
    is OtaDownloaded -> lwm2m-fw-state-value FwDownloaded
    is OtaVerifying -> lwm2m-fw-state-value FwDownloaded
    is OtaVerified -> lwm2m-fw-state-value FwDownloaded
    is OtaActivating -> lwm2m-fw-state-value FwUpdating
    is OtaRollingBack -> lwm2m-fw-state-value FwUpdating
    is OtaFailed (reason) -> lwm2m-fw-state-value FwIdle

  fw-result-resource : Lwm2mFw -> Integer
  fw-result-resource (c) = c.fw-result

  fw-package-uri : Lwm2mFw -> Text
  fw-package-uri (c) =
   when c.fw-ctx.manifest
    is None -> ""
    is Just (m) -> m.firmware-uri

Section: Write /5/0/1 -- Package URI

 The server writing the Package URI is what starts a download. The
 manifest carries the hash, the size and the sequence number, which the
 URI does not: those come from the release fact the publisher signed, and
 a device that took the server's word for them would let a compromised
 fleet server pick the image. The server carries bytes; the publisher
 grants execution.

  fw-write-package-uri : Lwm2mFw, OtaManifest -> Lwm2mFw
  fw-write-package-uri (c) (m) =
   let ctx2 = ota-step (c.fw-ctx) (EvManifestReceived m)
   in c revised {
    fw-ctx = ctx2,
    fw-written = 0,
    fw-next-block = 0,
    fw-faults = 0,
    fw-result = 0
   }

Section: The Download

 fw-next-request builds the CoAP GET for the block the client is waiting
 for. szx 6 is 1024-byte blocks, the largest RFC 7959 defines.

  fw-szx : Integer = 6

  fw-next-request : Lwm2mFw, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  fw-next-request (c) (msg-id) (token) =
   let path = fw-package-uri c
   in let blk = coap-block-option coap-opt-block2 (c.fw-next-block) 0 fw-szx
   in let opts = [coap-uri-path-option path, blk]
   in coap-build-request CoapConfirmable CoapGet msg-id token opts []

 A response is refused before it is staged. A packet that does not parse
 is not a short packet, it is not CoAP; a code that is not 2.05 Content
 is not a body; and a block that is not the one asked for is not the next
 block. Staging an out-of-order block would write the image at the wrong
 offset and leave a hole that Gate A would catch and nobody could
 explain, so it is refused where it happens.

  fw-feed-response : Lwm2mFw, List Integer -> [Flash, Device.Mmio] Lwm2mFw
  fw-feed-response (c) (bytes) =
   when coap-parse bytes
    is None -> fw-fail c fw-result-conn-lost "malformed CoAP response"
    is Just (m) ->
      if m.cm-code /= coap-205-content then fw-fail c fw-result-conn-lost "response was not 2.05 Content"
      else when coap-find-option (m.cm-options) coap-opt-block2
       is None -> fw-stage-last c (m.cm-payload)
       is Just (v) -> fw-stage-block c (m.cm-payload) (coap-uint-value v)

  fw-stage-block : Lwm2mFw, List Integer, Integer -> [Flash, Device.Mmio] Lwm2mFw
  fw-stage-block (c) (payload) (bv) =
   let num = coap-block-num bv
   in let more = coap-block-more bv
   in if num /= c.fw-next-block then fw-fail c fw-result-conn-lost "block out of order"
   else act
    c2 <- fw-write c payload
    if more == 1 then c2 revised { fw-next-block = num + 1 }
    else fw-download-complete (c2 revised { fw-next-block = num + 1 })
   end

  fw-stage-last : Lwm2mFw, List Integer -> [Flash, Device.Mmio] Lwm2mFw
  fw-stage-last (c) (payload) = act
   c2 <- fw-write c payload
   fw-download-complete c2
  end

 One block, one unlock-program-lock cycle. Holding a bank open across a
 network wait would leave a live write path into the image while the
 device sits idle, which is the state this whole design exists to avoid.
 The cost is a key sequence per block; the alternative is an unlocked
 bank owned by nobody.

  fw-write : Lwm2mFw, List Integer -> [Flash, Device.Mmio] Lwm2mFw
  fw-write (c) (payload) = act
   b0 <- flash-open-bank (c.fw-bank)
   b1 <- flash-write-page b0 (c.fw-stage + (c.fw-written)) payload
   f <- flash-seal-bank b1
   let ctx2 = ota-step (c.fw-ctx) (EvBlockReceived payload (c.fw-written))
   in c revised {
    fw-ctx = ctx2,
    fw-written = (c.fw-written) + list-length payload,
    fw-faults = (c.fw-faults) + (if f < 0 then 0 - f else 0)
   }
  end

Section: Gate A -- on what landed

 Gate A runs against the bank, not against a buffer the client kept, so a
 write the controller dropped fails here rather than being verified in
 absentia. sha256-buf hashes straight off the address in constant heap:
 the image is never a List, which is the memory honesty the design asks
 for on a 192 KB device.

 sha256-buf answers eight 32-bit WORDS, not thirty-two bytes.
 hkdf-words-to-bytes is the conversion, and skipping it is the bug that
 shipped a CCE hostname through TLS: a word list and a byte list are both
 List Integer and the type system cannot tell them apart, so the
 comparison would run and quietly answer nonsense.

  fw-digest : Lwm2mFw -> List Integer
  fw-digest (c) = hkdf-words-to-bytes (sha256-buf (c.fw-stage) 0 (c.fw-written))

  fw-magic-ok : Integer -> Boolean
  fw-magic-ok (addr) =
   if peek-byte addr 0 /= 67 then False
   else if peek-byte addr 1 /= 68 then False
   else if peek-byte addr 2 /= 88 then False
   else True

  fw-gate-a : Lwm2mFw -> GateAResult
  fw-gate-a (c) =
   when c.fw-ctx.manifest
    is None -> GateAFailTruncated
    is Just (m) ->
      if (c.fw-written) /= m.firmware-size then GateAFailTruncated
      else if fw-magic-ok (c.fw-stage) == False then GateAFailMagic
      else if ota-hash-eq (fw-digest c) (m.firmware-hash) 0 32 then GateAPass
      else GateAFailHash

 A staging fault outranks the hash. If a page write ran out of fuel the
 bank is short a page, and the honest answer is that flash failed
 (Result 2), not that the image was corrupt in transit (Result 5) -- the
 two send an operator to different places.

  fw-download-complete : Lwm2mFw -> Lwm2mFw
  fw-download-complete (c) =
   if (c.fw-faults) > 0 then fw-fail c fw-result-no-flash "flash write fault while staging"
   else when fw-gate-a c
    is GateAPass ->
      let ctx2 = ota-step (c.fw-ctx) (EvGateAResult GateAPass)
      in let ctx3 = ota-step ctx2 EvDownloadComplete
      in c revised { fw-ctx = ctx3 }
    is GateAFailMagic -> fw-fail c fw-result-bad-package "staged image is not a CDX"
    is GateAFailTruncated -> fw-fail c fw-result-conn-lost "staged image is short"
    is otherwise -> fw-fail c fw-result-integrity "staged image hash does not match the manifest"

Section: Execute /5/0/2 -- Update

 The server executing Update is what asks for Gate B. Gate B is trust and
 policy -- the author's key against the device's lattice, the manifest's
 capabilities against the device's grants -- and this chapter does not
 own any of that. It should not: a firmware client that carried the trust
 lattice would be a client that could be talked into changing it. So the
 verdict is computed by the caller, against the real verifier, and handed
 in. This chapter decides WHEN Gate B runs, which is what the design
 assigns it, and never what it decides.

 The verdict is delivered twice, and that is the machine's shape rather
 than a stutter. In OtaDownloaded the state machine answers ANY event by
 moving to OtaVerifying with action ActionRunGateB -- it is asking for
 the gate to be run, and it discards the verdict it was handed because it
 has not run it yet. Only the step taken in OtaVerifying reads the
 verdict. Sending it once leaves the device parked in Verifying forever,
 which the loopback caught by staging an image and then activating
 nothing.

  fw-execute-update : Lwm2mFw, GateBResult -> Lwm2mFw
  fw-execute-update (c) (verdict) =
   let ctx1 = ota-step (c.fw-ctx) (EvGateBResult verdict)
   in let ctx2 = ota-step ctx1 (EvGateBResult verdict)
   in when verdict
    is GateBPass -> c revised { fw-ctx = ctx2 }
    is otherwise -> fw-set-result (c revised { fw-ctx = ctx2 }) fw-result-update-failed

 Activation commits the candidate slot. The old firmware is still what
 boots until this point, which is the whole property: every refusal above
 leaves a device running the image it already trusted.

 Activation is legal from OtaVerified and nowhere else, and the guard is
 not decoration. The first cut of this function set Result to 1 (success)
 unconditionally, and the loopback reported a successful update that had
 staged zero bytes -- every block had been refused, the machine had never
 left Idle, and the device said Success anyway. A firmware client that
 can report success without installing anything is worse than one that
 fails, because the fleet stops looking. A state that is not Verified
 leaves the context and the Result exactly as the failure left them.

  fw-activate : Lwm2mFw -> Lwm2mFw
  fw-activate (c) =
   when c.fw-ctx.state
    is OtaVerified ->
      let ctx2 = ota-step (c.fw-ctx) EvActivationConfirmed
      in let ctx3 = ota-step ctx2 EvActivationConfirmed
      in fw-set-result (c revised { fw-ctx = ctx3 }) fw-result-success
    is otherwise -> c

  fw-boot-config : Lwm2mFw -> BootConfig
  fw-boot-config (c) = c.fw-ctx.boot-config

  fw-target-slot : Lwm2mFw -> BootSlot
  fw-target-slot (c) = ota-inactive-slot (c.fw-ctx.boot-config)
